<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">targeting</ansi>

Who each fighter aims at, and how a fight holds together.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Enemy groups</ansi>

Enemies that belong together (a band of bandits, a pack of wolves) fight as a
group, in a formation of two to five. Hostile creatures gather in groups:
the ones a place is known for spawn together, a rat and a ruffian if
that's what haunts the alley, never one alone. A lone survivor of a
broken group, or a straggler that wandered in, joins another idle group
there when it can; otherwise it lingers alone until the place brings it
company. Only a few great beasts always stand alone. A shopkeeper,
or someone you're talking with, isn't drawn into their group's fight.
Peaceful folk don't form groups: townsfolk who belong together each stand
alone, and are fought (if you must) one at a time, by name.

Each group has a name, shown in the room with its numbers: "A band of
ruffians (4): three ruffians and a rat." You fight a whole group, never
one member: <ansi fg="command">attack [group]</ansi> starts the battle (<ansi fg="command">attack ruffians</ansi>), and
<ansi fg="command">scout [group]</ansi> shows how it stands first. A creature alone goes by its
own name.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">One battle at a time</ansi>

You and your company fight one group at a time: that's your battle. Any
other group that turns on you waits its turn, and doesn't strike while it
waits. When your battle ends, the group that turned on you first begins
the next one at once.

While another player is free in the same place, a waiting group turns on
them instead ("The ruffian and the others turn toward Brom."), and you each
fight your own battle.

Once your battle starts, it plays out on its own: <ansi fg="command">attack</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">cast</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">backstab</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">shoot</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">break</ansi>, walking out, and changes to your
<ansi fg="command">formation</ansi> or <ansi fg="command">strategy</ansi>, eating, drinking, <ansi fg="command">use</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">equip</ansi>, and
<ansi fg="command">remove</ansi> are refused until it's over (type them again afterwards), and a
waiting group's spells hold off you. When you <ansi fg="command">flee</ansi>, only your battle's group can block
the way.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Keeping everyone fighting</ansi>

At the start of each round, while your company and an enemy group are
fighting:

- any fighter on your side with no target, a fallen target, or a target
  they can't reach turns to the foe their strategy picks among those they
  can reach (<ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>: the weakest, unless you set another rule,
  or your company's focus, <ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>);
  an enemy turns toward whichever of you it can reach its kind prefers
  (below);
- a fighter whose target is still in reach keeps it (a companion on
  <ansi fg="command">assist</ansi> or <ansi fg="command">defend</ansi> turns with what it follows);
- the enemy group's anger at you doesn't wear off while the fight lasts.

You're told when you turn (<ansi fg="command">You turn toward the bandit slinger.</ansi>), and see
when others do (<ansi fg="command">Garrick Vane turns toward the bandit bruiser.</ansi>).

A focus you call in a battle turns everyone at once at the next round,
even from a foe still standing (<ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>).

A fighter who can reach no one waits, and strikes as soon as someone comes
into reach (when a front fighter falls, for example).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Enemy personalities</ansi>

An enemy keeps a target it can reach. When it picks a new one (it joins
the fight, or its target falls or is out of reach), it picks the way its
kind does, among those of you it can reach:

  wolves and dogs      the most badly hurt (now and then, anyone)
  the undead           the nearest, always
  goblins and imps     your healers and casters: one chanting first
  spiders and insects  the weakest (often, anyone)
  people               the weakest (now and then, anyone)

Anything else goes for the weakest of you, as it always has. "Now and
then" is one pick in ten; "often" one in four; goblins one in seven.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Stepping out</ansi>

Only <ansi fg="command">flee</ansi> takes you out of a battle: it tries to run out of the room,
and your companions follow you. A flight that gets away ends your battle.
(<ansi fg="command">break</ansi> still stops a fight with another player.) A fight that isn't a
battle ends when nobody is fighting any more.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Alone</ansi>

With no companion beside you, you still fight one group at a time, and a
group whose target fell turns on you. You're turned the same way your
companions are: when your target falls, you turn to the foe your strategy
picks among those you can reach.

Combat labels such as the first cutthroat and the second cutthroat stay
fixed throughout the battle, including after a death. They help you follow
target changes; you still begin with attack [group]. See
<ansi fg="command">help narration</ansi> for how names and pronouns read.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help scout</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help attack</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>.
